      "math_academy_id": "e-41",
      "problem": "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} \\, .$",
      "worked_solution": "We can sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y = \\dfrac 1 {x^3}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14247879/assets/2497877ff3bc4a10871103f04502252843e2bdf8a9460c9ae4b32ff5344e921a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^-)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} = -\\infty, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^+)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} = \\infty,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3}= \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$",
